# What 95% confidence means
subtitle: "Twenty trials, twenty intervals, one miss: what the 95 percent in a confidence interval promises — and what it doesn't."

## What 95% confidence means
use: ci_dance
with:
    draws: 20
    confidence: 0.95
    box:
      x: 40
      'y': 80
      w: 920
      h: 580

    text lab_true "true effect: 5" at.anchor top at.ref true_line anchor bottom hidden true
    portrait portrait_jerzy_neyman x 865 y 360 width 150 of "Jerzy Neyman" hidden true
    label lab0 "3.4 to 7.0" right attach_to ci_0 hidden true
    label lab19 "2.1 to 4.6" left attach_to ci_19 hidden true
    path ax_line #3a3530 points [[200.2,128],[844.2,128]] hidden true
    path tk0 #3a3530 points [[200.2,128],[200.2,120]] hidden true
    text tl0 "0" x 200.2 y 100 font_size 24 hidden true
    path tk2 #3a3530 points [[329,128],[329,120]] hidden true
    text tl2 "2" x 329 y 100 font_size 24 hidden true
    path tk4 #3a3530 points [[457.8,128],[457.8,120]] hidden true
    text tl4 "4" x 457.8 y 100 font_size 24 hidden true
    path tk6 #3a3530 points [[586.6,128],[586.6,120]] hidden true
    text tl6 "6" x 586.6 y 100 font_size 24 hidden true
    path tk8 #3a3530 points [[715.4000000000001,128],[715.4000000000001,120]] hidden true
    text tl8 "8" x 715.4000000000001 y 100 font_size 24 hidden true
    path tk10 #3a3530 points [[844.2,128],[844.2,120]] hidden true
    text tl10 "10" x 844.2 y 100 font_size 24 hidden true
    text ax_name "mmHg lower" x 522.2 y 66 font_size 26 hidden true
    group axis members ["ax_line","tk0","tl0","tk2","tl2","tk4","tl4","tk6","tl6","tk8","tl8","tk10","tl10","ax_name"] hidden true
    text tally "19 of 20" x 845 y 590 font_size 40 hidden true
    text tally2 "caught it" x 845 y 535 font_size 40 hidden true

    card title "What 95% confidence means"

    hide caption

What does '95 percent confidence' actually promise? Less than most people think. Suppose a pill truly lowers blood pressure by 5 points: this dashed line.
    draw true_line lab_true axis

Nobody gets to see that line. A trial measures some patients and reports an interval: here, 3.4 to 7.0 points.
    draw ci_0 lab0

    erase lab0

A second trial, with other patients, lands somewhere else, and so does the next. The truth stays put; the intervals move.
    draw ci_1 ci_2 ci_3 ci_4

Run twenty trials, and most of the intervals straddle the dashed line…
    draw ci_5 ci_6 ci_7 ci_8 ci_9 ci_10 ci_11 ci_12 ci_13 ci_14 ci_15 ci_16 ci_17 ci_18

…but this one, from 2.1 to 4.6, misses it entirely. No mistake by its authors, just an unlucky sample.
    draw ci_19 lab19

Nineteen of twenty caught the truth. That catch rate, over many repeats, is all that 95 percent means.
    group tallyg members ["tally","tally2"]

From the inside, the red trial looks exactly like the others: its authors are just as confident, and wrong.
    focus ci_19

So the 95 percent belongs to the method, not to any one interval. Once computed, an interval either holds the 5 or it doesn't.
    highlight true_line

'A 95 percent chance the truth is in my interval' pictures the truth wandering. The truth is fixed; it's the intervals that wander.
    move true_line lab_true path [[45,0],[-45,0],[0,0]]

Jerzy Neyman introduced confidence intervals in the 1930s, and defined them exactly this way: by how often the method catches the truth.
    draw portrait_jerzy_neyman

    erase portrait_jerzy_neyman

    quiz "A trial reports a 95% interval of 2 to 8 points. What does the 95% describe?"
        right: The method: across many trials, 95 in 100 such intervals catch the true effect. This one either does or doesn't, and nobody can tell which.
        + How often the method's intervals catch the truth
        * The chance the true effect is between 2 and 8
        * The share of patients who improved by 2 to 8
---
